linux-trackpad-driver for Debian
=================================

The package ships: /usr/bin/ltd, udev rules
(lib/udev/rules.d/99-linux-trackpad-driver.rules), systemd units
(lib/systemd/system/linux-trackpad-driver.service,
linux-trackpad-driver-reconnect@.service,
linux-trackpad-driver-resume.service), the system-sleep hook
(lib/systemd/system-sleep/ltd-resume), and the default config
(/etc/linux-trackpad-driver/config.toml, a conffile — local edits are
preserved across upgrades).

No auto-enable, no auto-start (policy choice, debconf-free)
------------------------------------------------------------
Installing the package never enables or starts the daemon:

  * debian/rules runs dh_installsystemd with --no-enable --no-start,
    so no unit is enabled at install and nothing is started.
  * debian/postinst only reloads systemd (daemon-reload) and udev
    (udevadm control --reload-rules), then prints how to enable.
  * No debconf template exists: there is nothing to ask, because the
    safe default (disabled) always applies.

Rationale: the daemon touches hardware (feature-report init on attach,
resume re-init). Starting it implicitly on install would change device
behaviour without admin consent. One explicit step enables it:

  systemctl enable --now linux-trackpad-driver.service

The reconnect template needs no enabling (udev instantiates it per
device). The resume service can additionally be enabled for an
idempotent re-init pass at boot; the post-resume trigger itself is the
system-sleep hook, which runs whenever installed and executable.
Activation contract: background one-shots (udev reconnect, sleep hook,
plain resume unit) without the main service running leave the hardware
alone and exit 0 — disabled stays off. Explicit forcing while stopped
is `ltd resume -f` / `ltd reconnect -f` run directly.

Removal (debian/prerm) disables the main and resume units so no enabled
state is left behind.
